The <Taylor expansion> about $a$ has coefficients given by the successive <derivatives> at $a$:
$$
f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^N\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k+R_N(x).
$$
When $f$ is $N+1$ times continuously differentiable on the interval between $a$ and $x$, <Taylor's theorem> gives $R_N(x)=f^{(N+1)}(\xi)(x-a)^{N+1}/(N+1)!$ for an intermediate point $\xi$. If the remainders tend to zero, this yields the infinite <Taylor series> $f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}f^{(k)}(a)(x-a)^k/k!$. The three <functions> below are <real analytic> near zero after taking the continuous extension in part (i).

= i
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

First expand the <exponential function> and divide by $x$:
$$
\frac{1-e^{-x}}x=1-\frac x2+\frac{x^2}6-\frac{x^3}{24}+O(x^4).
$$
Seek its reciprocal as $1+Ax+Bx^2+Cx^3+O(x^4)$. Equating coefficients in the product with this expression gives
$$
A-\frac12=0,\qquad B-\frac A2+\frac16=0,\qquad C-\frac B2+\frac A6-\frac1{24}=0.
$$
Hence $A=1/2$, $B=1/12$, $C=0$. The first three nonzero terms of the <Taylor expansion> are
$$
\boxed{\frac{x}{1-e^{-x}}=1+\frac x2+\frac{x^2}{12}+O(x^4).}
$$
Although the quotient is undefined at zero as written, its limit is one, and this <Taylor expansion> belongs to its continuous, <real analytic> extension.

= ii
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $g(x)=\tan(x+\pi/4)$. The <derivative> identity for <tangent> gives $g'=1+g^2$, so $g(0)=1$, $g'(0)=2$, and $g''(0)=2g(0)g'(0)=4$. Its first three nonzero <Taylor expansion> terms are therefore
$$
\boxed{\tan(x+\pi/4)=1+2x+2x^2+O(x^3).}
$$
The factor $1/2!$ on the second <derivative> is what turns $g''(0)=4$ into the coefficient $2$.

= iii
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use $d(\ln\sec x)/dx=\tan x$ and $\ln\sec0=0$. To obtain enough terms of <tangent>, write $t(x)=x+ax^3+bx^5+O(x^7)$; it is an <odd function>. The identity $t'=1+t^2$ gives
$$
1+3ax^2+5bx^4+O(x^6)=1+x^2+2ax^4+O(x^6),
$$
so $a=1/3$, $b=2/15$. Integrate this <Taylor expansion> term by term from zero to $x$:
$$
\boxed{\ln\sec x=\int_0^x\tan t\,dt=\frac{x^2}2+\frac{x^4}{12}+\frac{x^6}{45}+O(x^8).}
$$
There is no constant term, and only even powers occur because $\ln\sec x$ is an <even function> near zero. Thus reaching the first three nonzero terms requires expansion through degree six.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The <comparison test for series> states that if $0\leq a_n\leq b_n$ for all sufficiently large $n$, then convergence of $\sum b_n$ implies convergence of $\sum a_n$; conversely, divergence of $\sum a_n$ implies divergence of $\sum b_n$. A summable bound on $|a_n|$ proves <absolute convergence> of $\sum a_n$.

For the <ratio test>, suppose the terms are eventually nonzero and $L=\lim_{n\to\infty}|a_{n+1}/a_n|$ exists. If $L<1$, the <series> converges absolutely. If $L>1$, including $L=\infty$, the terms fail to tend to zero and the <series> diverges. If $L=1$, the test is inconclusive. An eventually zero <series> converges separately. Convergence means that the sequence of partial sums has a finite limit; the terms tending to zero is only a necessary condition for a <convergent series>.

= i
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For every real $\alpha$, $|\cos(\alpha n)/n^2|\leq1/n^2$. The bounding <P-series> with exponent two converges. The <comparison test for series> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\text{absolute convergence for every }\alpha\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Oscillations of <cosine> are unnecessary for convergence here.

= ii
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

This is a <geometric series> with ratio $r=2\alpha/(\alpha^2+1)$. Since $\alpha^2+1>0$, the condition $|r|<1$ is equivalent to
$$
\alpha^2+1-2|\alpha|=(|\alpha|-1)^2>0.
$$
It holds for every real $\alpha$ except $\alpha=\pm1$. At $\alpha=1$ the terms are all one; at $\alpha=-1$ they alternate between $-1$ and $1$. Neither sequence of terms tends to zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{convergent exactly for }\alpha\in\mathbb R\setminus\{-1,1\}.}
$$
All the convergent cases have <absolute convergence>, including $\alpha=0$, when every term is zero.

= iii
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $a_n=n^2e^{-\alpha n}$, the <ratio test> gives
$$
\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(1+\frac1n\right)^2e^{-\alpha}=e^{-\alpha}.
$$
This is less than one exactly when $\alpha>0$. For $\alpha=0$ the terms are $n^2$, and for $\alpha<0$ they grow exponentially; in both cases they fail to tend to zero. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{convergent exactly for }\alpha>0.}
$$

= iv
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Rationalize the difference to reveal its small size instead of comparing its two large summands separately:
$$
0\leq\sqrt{n^4+\alpha^4}-n^2=\frac{\alpha^4}{\sqrt{n^4+\alpha^4}+n^2}\leq\frac{\alpha^4}{2n^2}.
$$
For any fixed real $\alpha$, the right-hand <series> is a constant multiple of a convergent <P-series>. The <comparison test for series> proves
$$
\boxed{\text{convergence for every }\alpha\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The case $\alpha=0$ is the identically zero <series>. For nonzero $\alpha$, its summand is asymptotic to $\alpha^4/(2n^2)$, explaining the same convergence behavior as the inverse-square <series>.

